WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:07.040
Moving from 3-space to 2-space&nbsp;
we're going to lose all depth information.&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:13.680
This is no surprise to us, we're accustomed to it.&nbsp;
If you take a picture of me, my 3-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.200
head becomes 2-dimensional, but the&nbsp;
immersion from my 3-dimensional head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:26.320
into 2-space - the flat screen that I'm projected&nbsp;
onto - loses all information of the back of my head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:37.760
and some part of my head becomes an edge. So a&nbsp;
photograph is a 2-dimensional immersion of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:44.640
3-dimensional object. Well a Klein&nbsp;
bottle is a manifold that really is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.800
4-dimensional. And it's a fairly accurate&nbsp;
representation of a Klein bottle almost everywhere&nbsp;-

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.320
almost everywhere. Well, not really, because first&nbsp;
of all it has a thickness. Glass has a thickness.&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.680
It's also not a perfect Klein bottle&nbsp;
because a perfect Klein bottle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:15.840
not only has zero thickness but its size&nbsp;
is undefined until a until Mr Riemann's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:23.360
metric is defined on it. To move this from four&nbsp;
dimensions to three it has to self-intersect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:33.680
This self-intersection is a result of it being&nbsp;
an immersion. There's several ways to do that, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.160
cause this self-intersection to happen. One -
the way I do it - is to bend the glass around, &nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.720
make a ring weld here, make a ring weld there,&nbsp;
make a weld here so that it's possible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:54.160
pour water in or make a connection through&nbsp;
here and get from the outer universe to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:01.440
sort of inside. 
Can you put water in a Klein&nbsp;bottle? You betcha in our physical universe.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.560
Yeah I got that, but there's other ways to do it.&nbsp;
So I talked to George Chittenden over at Adams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:15.680
and Chittenden Glass. George says, ah let me at&nbsp;
it! So he said another way to do it to make this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:26.960
almost reach there and this intersection&nbsp;
almost reach there. This is a valid immersion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:36.960
of a Klein bottle. I can go here, I can push&nbsp;
this up - oops - and you can kind of see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:47.840
it doesn't touch. This immersion allows an ant to&nbsp;
walk around here everywhere but it adds two edges -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:55.280
1,2 - that don't exist in the original&nbsp;
Klein bottle. The original Klein bottle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.600
has no edges and one side; this has one side

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:10.400
but two edges. What's been lost from the true Klein&nbsp;
battle into here is two edges have been introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.793
and it's no longer connected from inside&nbsp;to outside. 
- (Brady: What advantage does it have&nbsp;over your?)

00:03:15.793 --> 00:03:24.400
This has the huge advantage, as George&nbsp;
Chittenden said, of being slightly easier to make.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:35.840
But then George says, maybe I could try doing&nbsp;
another one where instead of having the break here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:45.360
let's make the break along here. Here we&nbsp;
have the neck going all the way through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.880
and a large hole around here - let's see if&nbsp;
I can show that hole. Okay, hope that shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:58.560
there's a hole that George burnt into the&nbsp;
side of the Klein bottle and he brings this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.329
tube right through it. 
- (That's kind of what you do&nbsp;in the other one except that hole then shrinks)&nbsp;

00:04:04.329 --> 00:04:12.880
(to touch it doesn't it?)
- Correct, exactly right Brady. In my normal Klein model I'll shrink this hole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.960
until it touches - what a glass blower would call&nbsp;
a ring weld - however these are slightly different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:26.240
For example, here, this is a genuine edge. If the&nbsp;
glass becomes arbitrarily thin this becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:36.960
razor sharp. Here it's not an edge, rather it's a&nbsp;
place where the manifold has multiple definitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:45.360
at one point. Here, on this Klein bottle, every&nbsp;
point is properly defined in the entire manifold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.720
Here that's almost true except&nbsp;
around the circle of singularities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.200
each point is defined as, oh it might be&nbsp;
part of this or it might be part of this.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:06.560
So I can spoodle this on this one, I&nbsp;
can also spoodle in, however this hole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.840
is a different approach to having an immersion.&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:21.440
Finally George said, well you know we could do it&nbsp;
so that we demonstrate that the surface of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:31.440
bottle is continuous even along here. Problem that&nbsp;
this has is there's a hole on the inside. So he put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:43.120
a window in there so it almost looks like this,&nbsp;
however there's a glass window that prevents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:52.160
a connection between here and there. So&nbsp;
in a sense this manifold is continuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:58.560
across here and continuous across here&nbsp;
but it still has the self intersection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.320
of a normal Klein bottle. All of these are&nbsp;
different immersions of the same thing; the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:13.680
point is you take a picture of my face or you&nbsp;
take a picture from behind me or from over here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:20.480
from up there, you're going to get a different view -
none of them are completely accurate. Is this a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:26.160
completely accurate picture of Felix Klein? No,&nbsp;
I can't tell what the back of his head is like!&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:32.080
I can't- I don't know how many ears he has. I know&nbsp;
he's got one ear, I can extrapolate to say he's got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:38.080
a second ear. The 3-dimensional human being&nbsp;
can't be seen in a 2-dimensional photograph,&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.560
the nature of an immersion. Of course with all&nbsp;
these different immersions of the Klein bottle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.600
my friend Lucas Clarke came by and he&nbsp;
said, Cliff I've got another approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:58.080
Lucas looked at a Coca-Cola bottle and he&nbsp;
said, I'm going to turn it into a Klein bottle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:09.200
And is that possible to see that this is- the very&nbsp;
end of the - this is where the cap goes - he's drawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:19.280
the neck of the Klein bottle through a hole -&nbsp;
mind you he has made a hole like this guy here -

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:30.240
and he's drawn the neck through and welded the lip&nbsp;
of the coke bottle to the base of the coke bottle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:38.560
What's astonishing about this is&nbsp;
not just the mathematical immersion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:50.240
but glass people will recognize that this is&nbsp;
a borosilicate glass, meaning that it's a very low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:58.400
expansion glass. All the- all my Klein bottles&nbsp;
are boros and it allows you to heat one area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:08.240
without heating the entire thing. Borosilicate&nbsp;
is relatively easy to work with, it's called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:14.960
hard glass because it doesn't expand much with&nbsp;
temperature, it's less likely to crack. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:23.280
coke bottle glass, it's the cheapest soda-lime&nbsp;
glass that the Coca-Cola company can find. Lucas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:32.400
bent all of this using soda-lime glass which&nbsp;
is really impressive but wait, there's even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:41.200
impressiveness about it. He was able to do all of&nbsp;
this glasswork and preserve the paint on it. Notice,&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:54.320
glassblowers would- can put labels on glass&nbsp;
using a ground-up ceramic called a ceramic frit.&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:09:00.400
Coca-Cola doesn't use a frit they just spray&nbsp;
paint it on or screen it on. Lucas Clarke not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:09.840
only was able to drill the hole but he was able to&nbsp;
preserve the label and do all of this glass work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.240
while retaining that it's a Coca-Cola bottle.&nbsp;
- (That's the actual original top that he's threaded)&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:24.000
(through the hole?)
- Yes it is but I suspect that he welded&nbsp;another piece onto here, in other words I suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:31.520
but I don't know, he didn't- Lucas keep secrets&nbsp;
from me. I suspect that he put an additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:38.720
maybe oh 30 centimeters of Coca-Cola&nbsp;
bottle glass to make this work

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.720
but I'm not going to ask his secrets,&nbsp;
there's some things I don't want to know.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:53.040
And held under water long enough...
- (So you're&nbsp;doing like a sort of a 360 are you to get)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:10:03.840
(the bubble out?)
- This hole has got&nbsp;to be the same as this hole! Oh my God

